%I A400384 #27 Oct 22 2026 14:36:43
%S A400384 4,8,9,16,18,32,36,64,72,100,128,144,162,196,200,256,288,324,392,400,
%T A400384 450,512,576,648,784,800,882,900,968,1024,1152,1296,1352,1458,1568,
%U A400384 1600,1764,1800,1936,2048,2178,2304,2450,2500,2592,2704,2916,3042,3136,3200,3528,3600,3872
%N A400384 Positive integers whose divisors can be partitioned into two disjoint subsets with sums differing by 5.
%C A400384 The sequence was first described in a problem in the International Gamma Mathematical Olympiad 2025.
%C A400384 This sequence is a generalization of Zumkeller numbers (A083207), which is defined as positive integers whose divisors can be partitioned into two disjoint sets with equal sum.
%C A400384 If k is in the sequence, the sum of its divisors sigma(k) must be odd, which implies that k must be either a perfect square or twice a perfect square.
%C A400384 A positive integer k is in this sequence if there exists a subset D of the divisors of k such that |2 * Sum_{d in D} (d) - sigma(k)| = 5.
%C A400384 Ting Hon Stanford Li (2026) proved that there exist infinitely many even positive integers and infinitely many odd positive integers in this sequence.
%H A400384 Alois P. Heinz, <a href="/A400384/b400384.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400384 Ting Hon Stanford Li, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13890">On a Generalization of Zumkeller Numbers: Infinitude of odd k-IGMO numbers for all non-negative integers k</a>, arXiv:2608.13890 [math.GM], 2026.
%e A400384 For k = 4: The divisors are {1, 2, 4}, and sigma(4) = 7. We can partition the divisors into D = {1} and D' = {2, 4}. The sum of D is 1 and the sum of D' is 6. The absolute difference is |1 - 6| = 5, so 4 is in the sequence.
%e A400384 For k = 8: The divisors are {1, 2, 4, 8}, and sigma(8) = 15. We can partition the divisors into D = {1, 4} (sum = 5) and D' = {2, 8} (sum = 10). The absolute difference is |5 - 10| = 5, so 8 is in the sequence.
%e A400384 For k = 9: The divisors are {1, 3, 9}, and sigma(9) = 13. We can partition the divisors into D = {1, 3} (sum = 4) and D' = {9} (sum = 9). The absolute difference is |4 - 9| = 5, so 9 is in the sequence.
%p A400384 q:= proc(n) option remember;
%p A400384       local l:= sort([numtheory[divisors](n)[]]);
%p A400384       local ll:= ListTools[PartialSums](l), b:=
%p A400384       proc(m, i) option remember; i=0 and m=0 or
%p A400384         (i>0 and m<=ll[i] and (b(abs(m-l[i]), i-1) or b(m+l[i], i-1)))
%p A400384       end; b(5, nops(l))
%p A400384     end:
%p A400384 select(q,[$1..4000])[];  # _Alois P. Heinz_, Oct 10 2026
%Y A400384 Subsequence of A028982.
%Y A400384 Cf. A083207 (Zumkeller numbers), A103977, A379503.
%K A400384 nonn,easy,new
%O A400384 1,1
%A A400384 _Li Ting Hon Stanford_, Sep 24 2026
